{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789314322,"nonce":"N3XrnE5ilUbl9c5TNOKUTYlhTc4=","title":"LICM hoisted a global's load out of a loop that called into a function storing to it","message":"Reproducer (Python frontend):\n\n    xs = [1, 2, 3]\n    total = 0\n    try:\n        i = 0\n        while i \u003c 10:\n            total += xs[i]\n            i += 1\n    except IndexError:\n        print(\"caught\", total, i)\n\nCPython prints `caught 6 3`. Once the inliner admitted `zb_list_get_i64` into the loop, its bounds check called `zb_hook_raise`, which stores the raised exception to the global `py$exc`. The loop's own check `py$exc != null` is a Load of that global; LICM's per-Load alias check found no Store to it in the loop body (the Store lives in the callee) and hoisted the Load to the preheader, so the loop never saw the exception and ran to the end: `caught 13 10`.\n\nThe per-Load alias check is sound only against the stores it can see. A call in the loop to anything not known pure may write any memory a pointer reaches, and no Store for it is in the body. Fix in `licm::hoist_loop`: when the loop contains a call to a function not inferred pure, a non-inline-safe intrinsic, a symbol, an indirect or closure call, an effect, an atomic or a fence, no Load is hoisted from it. Loads in loops with only pure calls (or none) hoist as before.\n\nConformance: `exceptions/raised_in_loop.py` pins the IndexError, KeyError and ZeroDivisionError cases raised from library code inside `while` and `for` loops.\n\nVerified the ZynML nbody and nbody_ref kernels interleaved A/B against HEAD: no change outside noise (nbody_ref's hot loop calls nothing impure once inlined; its loads still hoist).","files":null}]}